<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span>Operator Evolution During Training. The system automatically generates fused operators while maintaining a stable pool of active operators through intelligent elimination.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS5.4" class="ltx_text">IV-E</span> </span><span id="S4.SS5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Transferability and Generalization</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Cross-dataset experiments demonstrate strong transferability. Models trained on GSM8K transfer to MATH with only 1.2% performance degradation, while maintaining 85% of the cost reduction benefits. Cross-LLM evaluation using Claude-3.5-Sonnet and GPT-4 shows consistent improvements, validating the method’s model-agnostic nature.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conclusions and Future Work</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper introduces AutoMaAS, a novel framework for self-evolving multi-agent architecture search that bridges neural architecture search with large language model optimization. Our automated machine learning approach addresses fundamental limitations of existing approaches through four key innovations: dynamic operator lifecycle management, multi-objective cost optimization, online feedback integration, and enhanced interpretability mechanisms.</p>
</div>
